{
  "description": "Mint-response classification: how a token endpoint's reply becomes an enabled/disabled MintResult or a typed mint error. Derived from packages/core/src/mintHelpers.ts (mintResultFromResponse / mintResultFromFetch) and the Retry-After clamp in packages/core/src/engineTypes.ts (MintRateLimited constructor); every row below mirrors a row of the table in packages/core/tests/mintHelpers.test.ts, which was written vector-shaped for exactly this purpose. Why it is a cross-SDK contract: an identical 429 must show the same wait in every host, and a disabled avatar must never read as a failure. Row shape: `status` + `headers` + `body` in, `expect` out. `body.raw` is the JSON SOURCE TEXT the endpoint would put on the wire (the runner parses it, so malformed rows fail the way real ones do); `body.readFails` marks the one failure no body text can produce, a body that cannot be read at all (consumed or terminated), which must classify as `unreadable body` rather than as a parse failure. `bodyMustNotBeRead: true` means the STATUS ALONE decides the row and the implementation must not touch the body — a runner asserts the body was read 0 times, and every other row asserts exactly once (a real body is single-use). `detailExact` pins the wording where the wording is the contract; `detailContains` is the looser check every unavailable row carries. `via` names the entry point and DEFAULTS TO \"response\" when absent: \"response\" classifies an already-arrived response, \"fetch\" starts one step earlier from the pending request, and an implementation with only one entry point runs every row through it. `requestFailed: true` is the row where the request never produced a response at all (DNS, refused connection, TLS, CORS, abort). Two rules are decisions rather than mechanics and must not be \"cleaned up\": only an explicit `enabled: false` disables (an ABSENT flag means the endpoint does not have one, which is the livealman token endpoint), and a 429 is TERMINAL — nothing retries or reschedules it, because the server-side silent-session guard depends on hosts not retrying behind the user's back.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "200_enabled_true_with_url_token_room",
      "status": 200,
      "body": {
        "raw": "{\"enabled\":true,\"url\":\"wss://lk.example.com/rtc\",\"token\":\"jwt-header.jwt-payload.jwt-signature\",\"room\":\"pp-krystal-42\"}"
      },
      "expect": {
        "resolves": {
          "kind": "enabled",
          "url": "wss://lk.example.com/rtc",
          "token": "jwt-header.jwt-payload.jwt-signature",
          "room": "pp-krystal-42"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "200_enabled_false_is_disabled_not_an_error",
      "comment": "Chat is turned off here. A real outcome, not a failure.",
